#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# next_prime

#% text
# `next_prime(x)` gives the least prime greater than `x`.

#% text
# `next_prime(x, k)` gives the `k`-th prime after `x`, or the `-k`-th before it when `k < 0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `x` may be any real number; primality is decided for integers of any size (with a strong probable-prime test beyond the proven range).

#% item
# `next_prime` is listable in its first argument.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
next_prime(10)
#% output
#= 11

#% cell
next_prime(13)
#% output
#= 17

#% cell
next_prime(100, 3)
#% output
#= 107

#% cell
next_prime(100, -1)
#% output
#= 97

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A real starting point:

#% cell
next_prime(2.5)
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# A large integer:

#% cell
next_prime(10^30)
#% output
#= 1000000000000000000000000000057

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
next_prime([1, 10, 100, 1000])
#% output
#= [2, 11, 101, 1009]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The first prime gap of length at least 10:

#% cell
p = 2; while next_prime(p) - p < 10 { p = next_prime(p) }; [p, next_prime(p)]
#% output
#= [113, 127]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `prime`, `is_prime`, `prime_pi`
